1. The Pause Button That Became a Starting Pistol

On 22 March 2023, the Future of Life Institute published an open letter calling for every AI laboratory to pause for at least six months the training of systems more powerful than GPT-4. It described the industry as an uncontrolled race and said decisions of this magnitude should not be delegated to unelected technology leaders. Musk was among the prominent signatories. 

On 12 July 2023—less than four months later—Musk publicly launched xAI as a competitor to OpenAI and the other major laboratories. 

Strictly speaking, this does not prove that Musk violated the letter. The letter concerned training beyond GPT-4’s capability, and the first version of Grok was not demonstrated to exceed GPT-4.

The more interesting asymmetry is strategic.

The existing leaders were being asked to freeze advancement at the frontier while Musk assembled a new entrant below it.

The proposed market arrangement looked like:

OpenAI, Google and Anthropic:
Pause frontier scaling.
Elon Musk:
Build team, infrastructure, data access,
product integration and competing model.

This may have been entirely sincere. It was also extremely convenient.

A six-month freeze imposed on incumbents would not have stopped AI development generally. It would have reduced the rate at which the existing leaders widened their advantage while a new competitor organised itself.

The pause letter said the race had become dangerously uncontrolled.

Musk’s practical response was not to leave the stadium.

It was to request a delay while fastening his own number to the car.

