2. The Referee Who Owns a Team

In September 2023, Musk told US lawmakers that AI required a referee to ensure companies acted safely and in the public interest. At the UK AI Safety Summit two months later, he again called for a third-party referee capable of examining frontier developers and raising concerns. 

By July 2026, his proposal had shifted.

Musk now advocated regular meetings among leading AI companies and peer review of one another’s frontier models before release. Under this arrangement, government would step in only when a company ignored serious concerns identified by the other companies. 

That is not an independent referee.

That is the teams examining one another’s boots in the tunnel and calling the league only when somebody finds a knife.

The progression is:

2023:
Government needs a referee.
2023:
The referee should be independent.
2026:
The leading AI companies can review one another.
Government:
Reserve official, summoned after the companies disagree.

Competitor review may be technically useful. Frontier laboratories often understand the systems better than generalist regulators.

But an arrangement in which OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, Meta and xAI inspect one another is not external public oversight. It is an industry club exchanging technical objections while remaining commercially invested in the same race.

Musk is also no neutral spectator within that club.

SpaceX’s prospectus says that Musk currently controls the company and, under the proposed offering assumptions, would retain approximately 83.6% of its total voting power, while the Class B stock would elect 51% of authorised directors. The company would remain a controlled company under Nasdaq governance rules. 

The proposed AI referee therefore owns:

* one of the teams;
* the training facility;
* the media platform covering the match;
* the communications network serving parts of the audience;
* and more than four-fifths of the voting power over the combined corporate stadium.

This is less FIFA than one man buying football, the television rights and most of Switzerland.

