5. The Lawsuit Is Also Content

Musk’s fight with OpenAI has moved through public accusations, multiple lawsuits, injunction attempts and an unsolicited consortium offer of $97.4 billion for the nonprofit controlling OpenAI. OpenAI argued in court that the bid conflicted with Musk’s position that the organisation’s assets should remain dedicated to charitable purposes; Musk disputed OpenAI’s conduct and maintained that it had abandoned its founding mission. The merits have been heavily contested. 

The legal merits are one question.

The competitive function is another.

Every filing:

* forces OpenAI to respond;
* frames the history of the organisation in public;
* generates news coverage;
* turns Musk and Altman into opposing characters;
* reminds customers that xAI exists;
* and keeps the industry’s leading firms engaged in a permanent public contest.

Litigation can therefore create value even before a court determines anything.

V_{\text{lawsuit}}
=
V_{\text{legal outcome}}
+
V_{\text{competitive delay}}
+
V_{\text{narrative attention}}

A lawsuit need not be frivolous to perform all three functions.

The OpenAI dispute has become part contract argument, part corporate-governance battle and part recurring television series in which every participant sells AI subscriptions during the adverts.

Musk may sincerely believe OpenAI betrayed its mission.

He is also the chief executive of a direct competitor.

The courtroom is not outside the race.

It is another lane.

