7. The New Pipes Thesis, Stated Correctly

Not every AI operation requires the internet.

Local models can run offline. Edge devices can perform inference without continuous cloud access. Terrestrial data-centre traffic is not about to abandon fibre and travel through Starlink by default.

The stronger argument is more specific.

As commercial AI expands, companies are trying to place agents and model-enabled services into:

* vehicles;
* ships;
* aircraft;
* farms;
* construction sites;
* remote industrial equipment;
* military systems;
* disaster areas;
* mobile devices;
* and infrastructure beyond reliable terrestrial coverage.

Those systems do not all require continuous connectivity to think.

They require connectivity to receive updates, obtain current information, call larger models, coordinate with other systems, send evidence, upload telemetry and remain under remote supervision.

Starlink is positioned precisely at that access boundary.

As of 31 March 2026, SpaceX reported approximately 10.3 million Starlink subscriber lines across 164 markets. It also operates satellite-to-mobile services intended to reduce conventional mobile dead zones, with SpaceX saying it began deploying Direct-to-Cell capability in January 2024. 

The commercial relation is:

N_{\text{connected AI endpoints}}
\uparrow
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
D_{\text{remote connectivity}}
\uparrow

Not every additional AI device becomes a Starlink customer.

But every expansion of intelligent systems into places where terrestrial connectivity is absent, unreliable or politically vulnerable enlarges the market Starlink is designed to serve.

Musk does not need to own every internet packet.

He needs to own enough of the final impossible mile.

The AI laboratories are building the new workers.

Musk increasingly owns the road to the remote factory.

