12. Safety at the Speed of Turbine Installation

Musk repeatedly advocates caution, oversight and model review.

xAI simultaneously promotes speed as a defining operating advantage. It says Colossus was built in 122 days and expanded to 200,000 GPUs in another 92 days. 

The physical infrastructure required to support that speed has produced a less philosophical dispute.

A Reuters investigation reported in July 2026 that xAI had installed 59 natural-gas turbines associated with its Colossus 2 project without federal clean-air permits. xAI and Mississippi regulators have argued that the temporary or portable turbines are exempt; environmental and civil-rights groups dispute that interpretation, and litigation remains active. Reuters calculated potentially substantial emissions near predominantly Black communities already experiencing relatively high respiratory-disease burdens. 

The legal question is not settled.

The engineering pattern is visible:

Compute demand arrives.
Power infrastructure is installed rapidly.
Regulatory classification is argued afterward.

This is an awkward physical counterpart to the call for an AI referee.

When the proposed restraint concerns rival models, the world requires review, coordination and possibly government intervention.

When the restraint concerns the power plant feeding Musk’s own model, the turbines are already humming while lawyers debate whether they count.

The referee appears to have been delayed by grid connection.

