14. The Warning About Unelected Technology Leaders

The 2023 pause letter said decisions about powerful AI should not be delegated to unelected technology leaders. 

SpaceX’s prospectus says Musk currently holds 84.3% of its voting rights and would retain approximately 83.6% following the contemplated public offering assumptions. It also provides Class B holders with the ability to elect a majority of authorised directors. 

Inside that controlled company now sit:

* SpaceX launch;
* Starlink connectivity;
* xAI compute;
* Grok;
* and X.

This gives the original warning an unfortunate sequel:

Decisions this important must not be delegated to unelected technology leaders—unless the leader holds enough super-voting stock to make delegation unnecessary.

There is nothing automatically illegitimate about founder control in a private company.

The contradiction arises when the controlled company’s founder presents concentrated private technological authority as a public danger while consolidating several of the most strategically important layers under his own vote.

The proposed antidote to unelected AI power is apparently one particularly energetic unelected AI owner.

